Track 3: Sound-Head Slack-Water
- Story & Context: By the late autumn of 1984, municipal authorities had finally bulldozed the Cine Marajó into the brackish shallows of Ponta da Praia, but the crew dragged two gasoline generators and an ancient carbon-arc lantern onto an anchored, abandoned salt barge before Inspector Valadares could seize their gear. As the tide went slack, leaving the barge stranded in thick black harbor mud that reeked of fuel oil and rotting oysters, they projected thirty minutes of blank white leader across the channel directly onto the hull of an arriving container freighter, playing their final defiant suite against the moving steel wall. Dockers gathered along the wharf cranes in the damp sea mist to listen as Tiago's melodica shrieked like a wounded sea gull over an explosive 808 beat, with Zé barking the scene's ultimate myth into a battered megaphone: "The harbor drinks the celluloid, but the bassline keeps the dock employed!" Though the makeshift sound system was swallowed when the tide surged back at dawn, old sailors insist the harbor's water pumps still rattle in that exact syncopated shuffle.
